: first, that decentralized, competitive ecosystems produce superior epistemic and safety outcomes compared to centralized monopolies; second, that the cost of delaying AI progress—measured in lost medical breakthroughs, energy efficiency gains, and scientific discovery—far outweighs the hypothetical risks of unmanaged development. If these assumptions prove wrong—specifically, if the risks of AI are truly uncontrollable by decentralized actors while being perfectly manageable by states—then my argument for market-based governance collapses into a reckless gamble. However, history demonstrates that centralized control is the single point of failure in almost every technological transition. We must choose dynamic resilience over the fragile illusion of state-directed safety. 🚀",
