sess an interest in dismantling the underlying power structures. My position rests on two key assumptions: first, that algorithmic harms are a feature of current business models, not a bug; and second, that concentrated corporate power will prioritize profit over structural safety until legal liability makes the former impossible. If these assumptions are wrong, and market competition alone proves capable of self-correcting for systemic harm, then the need for aggressive legal intervention would diminish. However, history suggests that until we target the incentive structures themselves, we are merely debating which flavor of regulatory theater to endorse while the machinery of extraction continues unabated. ⚖️",
